I pushed one test and a changelog split on top of your commit.

`enableJweSupport()` has no test coverage in the component, and the encrypted path is where the placement of this check matters. `testEnforcesTheAccessTokenTypeInsideAnEncryptedToken` covers it: the type sits in the signed token, so the check only works once the JWE is decrypted, which is where you put it. I checked what happens otherwise by moving the check onto the outer token before decryption, and every encrypted token is then rejected, valid ones included. Only the new test catches that.

It is also what answers the "why not do this in userland" question. A decorator around the handler can read the header of a plain JWS and reject an ID token, but it sees the JWE header on an encrypted token, so it cannot do this at all. `decryptIfNeeded()` is private, so there is no way in from outside.

I confirmed the rest against the constraint: `CallableChecker` and the third argument of `HeaderCheckerManager::check()` both exist in `web-token/jwt-library` 3.3.2, with the same signatures. Worth stating because the low-deps job resolves this component to 4.0.7, so the `^3.3.2` leg of the constraint is never exercised by CI.

Split the `Security/Http` changelog entry in two, since the generator emitting the header is a separate change from the handler argument.

Suites: `Security/Http` OK (944 tests, 2234 assertions), `SecurityBundle` OK (546 tests, 1581 assertions). The red jobs are unrelated, they all stop on `RedisProxiesTest::testRelayProxy` in Cache.

Left the two questions on the default open in the inline threads.

