Thanks for the JWE test, it is the right one: it pins the placement of the check, and it is also the answer to "why not a decorator", since a decorator only ever sees the JWE header. Kept as is, along with the changelog split.

Both your questions are applied, and the two inline threads carry the details. In short:

- `$enforceAtJwtType` defaults to `true`. No FC layer in the component, and `UPGRADE-8.2.md` carries it as a BC BREAK under Security for direct instantiations.
- The `enforce_at_jwt_type` node is `defaultNull()`, and not setting it is deprecated, resolving to `false`. An application coming from 8.1 through SecurityBundle sees no behaviour change until 9.0.

One thing worth recording, since 8.2 is unreleased and that sometimes lets us skip a deprecation: it does not apply here. `OidcTokenHandler` shipped in 6.3 and has never read `typ` on any released version, 6.3 through 8.1. Only the option and the constructor argument are new, so their shape is free, but the enforcement cannot be turned on silently.

Test side, `buildJWS()` and `buildJWSWithKey()` now emit `at+jwt` by default and keep a header override for the rejection cases. `invalidAccessTokens` in the functional suite gained an ID token and a token with no type, both 401 end to end, and the `access_token_oidc` fixtures set the option so the deprecation is not tripped anywhere else.

Suites: `Security/Http` 944 tests, 2234 assertions, `SecurityBundle` 549 tests, 1591 assertions, both green with no unexpected deprecation. The red jobs are the `RedisProxiesTest::testRelayProxy` drift in Cache, which the 8.2 branch itself shows at the commit this is rebased on.
